body:
Adds `ai` to the observed-call contract, so a host takes an AI Logic call production answered and writes the AI event the sandbox's own AI calls already make, with the model and token counts and never any text.

```ts
import { observedCallEvent, readObservedCall } from 'pyric/sandbox/internal';

// What a client will send once live mode wraps firebase/ai (a later PR).
const call = readObservedCall({
  service: 'ai',
  method: 'generateContent',
  at: Date.now(),
  durationMs: 120,
  outcome: { status: 'ok' },
  projectId: 'demo-project',
  ai: {
    model: 'models/gemini-flash-lite-latest',
    usage: { promptTokenCount: 9, candidatesTokenCount: 2, totalTokenCount: 11 },
  },
});

observedCallEvent(call);
// {
//   kind: 'operation', service: 'ai', method: 'generateContent',
//   path: 'models/gemini-flash-lite-latest',              // the model is the path
//   rulesDisposition: { kind: 'not-evaluated', reason: 'external-execution' },
//   production: { outcome: { status: 'ok' } },
//   observation: {
//     id, startedAt, endedAt, status: 'completed',        // no `response`, ever
//     ai: { requestedModel: 'models/gemini-flash-lite-latest', engine: 'gemini',
//           endpoint: 'https://firebasevertexai.googleapis.com', usageSource: 'backend',
//           inputTokens: 9, outputTokens: 2, totalTokens: 11, durationMs: 120 },
//   },
// }
```

## The reader takes a model and numbers, and refuses everything else

```ts
readObservedCall({ ...call, ai: { ...call.ai, text: 'Hello' } });
// throws ObservedCallError: Invalid observed call field: ai.text.
readObservedCall({ ...call, ai: { ...call.ai, usage: { promptTokenCount: '9' } } });
// throws: Invalid observed call field: ai.usage.promptTokenCount.
readObservedCall({ service: 'firestore', method: 'getDoc', at: 1, outcome: { status: 'ok' }, ai: call.ai });
// throws: Invalid observed call field: ai.
```

- `ai` is required on every `ai` call and refused on any other service.
- `model` and `reportedModel` (production's `modelVersion`) match `^[A-Za-z0-9._/-]{1,200}$`.
- `usage` holds only `promptTokenCount`, `candidatesTokenCount`, `totalTokenCount` and `totalTokens` (from `countTokens`), each a safe integer of 0 or more.
- `firstChunkMs` is a time of 0 or more, set on a stream.
- `path` is refused on an `ai` call, since the event's path is the model.

Tokens go through the shared `aiUsageEvidence` mapping, so an observed call counts input, output and total the way the sandbox broker does. A `countTokens` call's `totalTokens` is the total. A call with no usage reads `usageSource: 'unknown'`. A refusal such as `{ status: 'error', code: 'unauthenticated' }` gives `result: 'error'`, `observation.status: 'failed'` and `observation.error.code`.

## Risk

Low. No client sends `service: 'ai'` yet, so no running app produces this event until live mode serves `firebase/ai`. Billed-call counts skip it, since `billedCallKind` counts only Firestore. `inbound-validation.ts` already reads every `observe` frame with `readObservedCall`, so the SharedWorker and the Node host take the new service with no change under `packages/cli/src/serve/`. The host test checks it through `handleMessage`.

`ObservedAi` is not re-exported from `pyric/sandbox`'s type index, to keep this change to its five planned files; a caller reads it as `NonNullable<ObservedCall['ai']>`.

Integration test: none. This change has no runtime path, because no client sends `ai` calls until the later PR that makes `firebase/ai` a live row. Its scenario is the integration evidence for the whole AI Logic plan.

<details>
<summary>Implementation notes</summary>

- `packages/pyric/test/sandbox/observed-call.test.ts` on main: 4 of 13 failed. The reader threw `Invalid observed call field: service.` for an `ai` call, and `observedCallEvent` built no observation.
- After: `bun test test/sandbox/observed-call.test.ts test/ai/ai-evidence.test.ts` in `packages/pyric`, 19 pass, 0 fail.
- `bun test test/serve/worker/host/observe.test.ts test/serve/live-host-state.test.ts` in `packages/cli`, 15 pass, 0 fail. The new host case expects an `ai.generateContent` event with `production.outcome`, input 9, output 2, total 11, `usageSource: 'backend'` and no `observation.response`, and the malformed list refuses `ai` on a Firestore call, a `text` key and a string token count.
- `tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit` and `tsc -p tsconfig.types.json` in `packages/pyric` pass.
- `AI_LOGIC_ENDPOINT` is a new constant in `sandbox/internal/ai-evidence.ts`.
- `observation.error.code` is set on a failed call, as the sandbox's AI events set it.
- The conformance coupling gate passes on a `Conformance-Exempt` trailer: the reader and event change, and no conformance row or served surface does.

</details>

Refs #2114

## What and why

Approvals used to pause a whole node: the approver never saw which tool would run or with which parameters. This PR adds a workspace rule that makes risky tools wait for a person on every call. The server enforces it at the point the call would execute.

## Design

- **Policy, not a per-step flag.** The org setting `tools.approvalRequired` lists the tool names that require approval. It lives in the existing org-config catalog and needs `org.config.write`.
  - The inspector adds or removes one tool through `POST /org/tool-approval-policy`, which edits the list under a per-org lock so concurrent admins never drop each other's entries.
  - `POST /org/config` refuses this key with `tool_approval_policy_path` and points to the add/remove path. Reading it there still works.
  - Edits are audited. A malformed stored value fails closed on read.
- **Inspector.** Admins get a switch: "Require approval for this tool in every workflow". Other members see a read-only "Requires approval (workspace policy)" pill.
- **Where it is enforced.** The dispatcher reads the policy for `tool`, `http`, `loop`, `agent` and `multi_agent` steps and matches the tool name actually rendered for the call; an `http` step is `http.request`. So a step id, a decoy step, a replayed fix, a rerun override or a Replay Lab patch cannot move a listed call out of the gate.
- **Where a listed tool can run.** Only as an approved `tool` or `http` step. If a `for_each` loop or an agent reaches it, including an agent's `http.request`, it is refused with `TOOL_APPROVAL_REQUIRED`.
- **Only enforceable names.** The setting accepts only built-in tool names, `http.request` included. MCP tools and unknown names are refused with `tool_approval_unenforceable`, so the setting never promises a gate that does not exist.
- **System deliveries.** Report and digest deliveries go through `Engine.ExecuteIntegrationTool`, which refuses a listed tool because nobody approves a system delivery.
  - Report delivery answers 409 `tool_approval_required`.
  - Listing `email.send` stops the weekly digest, and its `last_error` names `TOOL_APPROVAL_REQUIRED`.
  - A policy that cannot be read refuses as the retryable `TOOL_APPROVAL_POLICY_UNAVAILABLE`, and report delivery answers 503.
  - Alert notifications use the HTTP executor directly, so listing `http.request` does not hold them back.
- **Policy changes.** Once a step has a live request, that request decides it whatever the policy says later. Removing a tool does not run a denied or expired call, and an undecided request keeps waiting until it expires.
- **Credential binding under the lock.** The digest, credential revision included, is computed under the run lock. The approved call pins every credential it names, including one that was absent at approval. The integration refuses any credential whose revision changed after the spend, any credential that was created after it, and any credential the approved call never named.
- **Validation runs and draft tests.** They keep suppressing writes as before. The gate waits only for listed calls that would really run there, which are reads.
- **The request.** A listed tool step pauses before the executor runs, under the run's completion lock. The request stores:
  - the tool;
  - the masked parameters, plus the paths where values were masked;
  - an HMAC digest of the full rendered call, which also binds the named credential's id, kind, reference and revision;
  - the requesters;
  - the number of eligible approvers.
- **Who cannot decide.** The people who caused the call, recorded in `run_actors` by role:
  - anywhere in the run tree (the lineage up through subworkflow, rerun and replay links, plus every subworkflow descendant):
    - form submitters and resumes with input;
    - a redrive that replaced the document, whether through a replay fix, cluster apply or auto-healing;
    - whoever created or applied a semantic output replacement;
    - a service-token starter;
  - in this run and the runs above it: a plain redrive;
  - the creators up the lineage, and everyone an earlier request for the step excluded;
  - for a run that no person started, nor any subworkflow parent (a `system:*` creator, a schedule or webhook trigger, or a service token), whoever saved its version. A rerun or replay source does not count as that person.

  A service token's user id is a free-form header, so its actions count as `service-token:<suffix>`. Eligible approvers are members who can start runs and are not requesters. Approve and Deny show only to eligible users, and the card says "No eligible approver" when nobody qualifies.
- **Decision.** `POST /runs/tool-approvals/decide` requires `runs.start` and refuses service tokens. It refuses requesters (also with a database check), a stale digest, and a request past its 30-minute decision deadline. It serializes on the run lock, so the first decision wins, and repeating your own decision is a no-op.
- **Spend.** The next claim spends the decision exactly once.
  - An approval stays spendable for 30 minutes after it was given, even past the decision deadline, so a busy queue does not fail a timely approval.
  - A changed digest, a deny or an expiry fails the step with a non-retryable `ToolApprovalError`, which follows the step's error route.
  - A retry, redrive, replay or restart asks again, and a retry or redrive gives up an unspent approval.
- **Redrive operators** are recorded on every path: run panel, `/runs/redrive`, `/v1/dlq/redrive`, `/dlq/replay`, cluster apply, MCP `dlq.redrive` (with its principal), and replay campaigns (with the campaign's creator).
- **Generic resume** cannot complete a tool approval.
- **Schema.** Changes are additive to the single baseline: new tables `tool_approval_requests` and `run_actors`, and a plain `(run_id, node_id)` index.

## The per-step flag never shipped

Earlier rounds of this PR used a per-step `requireApproval` flag. It never reached main, so dropping it needs no migration and fails nothing open: no saved workflow can carry it.

## What the redesign removed

The per-step `requireApproval` flag and every document comparison that existed to protect it:
- the latest-saved-version check in `StartRun`;
- the snapshot comparisons on redrive, Replay Lab, fix validation and sample runs;
- the rerun-override and AI-patch guards;
- the id-change refusal.

Rollouts, older versions, webhooks with a captured version, older-run redrives, bulk replay and Replay Lab all work as they do on main.

## Screenshots (described, not attached)

Captured in an earlier round against my own stack.
- **Paused card, EN and ES.** It shows:
  - the tool and its exact parameters, with `authorization` shown as `[redacted]` (a DOM probe found no secret value on the page);
  - the policy facts and the decision deadline;
  - Approve and Deny.
- **Outcomes, verified in PostgreSQL.**
  - Approve ran the real webhook call, which failed against the unreachable test host and took the error route.
  - Deny failed the step with TOOL_APPROVAL_DENIED and took the error route.
- **Not re-captured.** This round changed the inspector control (the per-step switch became the workspace policy switch), added the masked-paths line, and added the "No eligible approver" state. Unit tests cover these; I did not take new screenshots of them.

## Tests

- **Engine integration (PostgreSQL 18, -race):**
  - the approved call runs once;
  - the starter, a stale digest, cross-org and generic resume are refused;
  - deny takes the error route with no retry;
  - expiry;
  - single use and parameter binding;
  - concurrent approvers produce one winner;
  - validation runs;
  - credential revision binding;
  - the spend window after the decision deadline;
  - a redrive retires and excludes its redriver;
  - masked paths;
  - the decoy step and the loop path are both gated by tool name;
  - older documents start, and older runs redrive and then wait;
  - requesters are only the people behind the exec

Targets `main` directly. **Merge after #49–#52.** Tests and docs only; production code is unchanged.

## What shipped
- **`R3AcceptanceTest`**: the full R3 journey over real HTTP:
  - split by items, by amount and into equal parts (33.34/33.33/33.33);
  - a partition document;
  - a mixed-payment printout, `Pagamento misto — Numerário: 100.00; A crédito: 50.00`, and `Saldo em aberto`;
  - a credit sale, a debt payment and the slip;
  - a refund with approver, a settled void within headroom, an absorbed tax cent (D96 M1) and a pending correction (M2);
  - an in-doubt payment: reclassify guard, forced close blocked, resolved by the poller;
  - cash close, reopen, adjust and rounds;
  - reconciliation;
  - reports with rebuild = live;
  - audit scope and export;
  - support access.
- **`RestartMatrixTest` (a–h)**: a server is killed at each crash point. A second server on the same database plus a client retry with the same session and Idempotency-Key gives exactly one effect.
- **`R3ReportBenchmark`** (run by name): 150k documents and 600k lines. The worst 31-day p95 is 258 ms (target 1 s). No rollups needed (D86).
- **Backlog test items**:
  - the missing coded-error contract entries (customers cases moved in);
  - the widened SettlementIssuer ArchUnit rule;
  - the S19 flake made deterministic;
  - a split property test with cancels, two rates and the pending path;
  - a snapshot-content IT for a partition document plus the residual.
- **Docs**: ADRs 0019–0027 (★ decisions plus D96–D98), CONTEXT.md terms, backend-conventions, regenerated module docs, and `docs/qa/` (acceptance matrix A16–A37, test plan, bug reports).
- **`deploy/test/real-schema-drill.sh`** seeds every R3 table through the API and passes in production (encrypted) mode, with 81 migrations.

## Gate evidence
- **Full suite**: 1742 tests, 0 failures, errors or skipped (txt = xml counts).
- **Drills, in production mode**: `test-backup-encryption.sh` (63 checks, including recovery-key-only), `test-backup-restore.sh`, `test-pitr.sh` full and quick (RPO 273 s, RTO 4 s), and the extended real-schema drill. All PASS.
- **Bugs filed, with reproducing tests**, both fixed in S33b:
  - BUG-0001: the em dash prints '?' on CP860, CP850, CP437 and CP858.
  - BUG-0002: the statement period ignores the business-day cutoff.

## Release readiness
These are not blocked by tests:
- **§14 #10**: a certified fiscal adapter and country validation are still needed (B4).
- **§14 #1**: two-installation isolation has no drill.
- **§14 #6**: the network-cut behaviour needs the POS frontend.
- **Hardware and load**: real printers (B5) and a concurrent-terminal R3 load test.

